            "old_string": "### 43.5 Starter bitmask pruning for context-clue pre-scan\n- [ ] The context-clue pre-scan (`clue_ac` in `scan/mod.rs`) uses a\n      bytewise Aho-Corasick on ~200-400 patterns to detect which rules\n      have active context clues in the text.  This runs on every scan,\n      even when most clue patterns have no possible match at the current\n      position.\n  - opencc-fmmseg's core insight: precompute a per-starter-character\n    bitmask of which pattern lengths exist.  At each text position,\n    query `bitmask[char]` — if zero, skip entirely.  If non-zero,\n    iterate only set bits (viable lengths) via `leading_zeros()`.\n    This reduces probe count from `O(max_len)` to `O(K)` where K is\n    the number of distinct lengths for that starter (~3-5 typical).\n  - For zhtw-mcp: the clue AC is already efficient (bytewise AC handles\n    ~400 patterns in microseconds).  The bigger win is applying the\n    starter-bitmask concept to the **spelling AC fallback path** —\n    the bytewise Aho-Corasick used when charwise daachorse build fails.\n    On the charwise path, daachorse already has good locality; the\n    bitmask would add overhead, not remove it.\n  - Concrete target: build `StarterMask` (dense `[u64; 65536]` for BMP)\n    at `Scanner::new()` time from the spelling rule `from` patterns.\n    Use it in the bytewise fallback's match validation to skip\n    impossible starters before substring extraction.\n  - Profile first: if 30.7 profiling shows clue pre-scan or bytewise\n    fallback is <5% of total CPU, close as won't-fix.\n  - Scope: `engine/scan/mod.rs` (StarterMask build), `engine/scan/\n    spelling.rs` (use in bytewise path).\n  - Gate: measurable speedup (>10%) on bytewise fallback path for\n    100KB input.  No change to charwise path.",
