            "old_string": "### 43.7 Delimiter-aware scan pre-segmentation\n- [ ] The scanner runs AC automata over the full text, including\n      punctuation, whitespace, and delimiter sequences where no rule\n      can possibly match.  AC handles this efficiently (transitions\n      through fail states), but delimiter-heavy text (e.g. Markdown\n      tables, YAML) wastes transitions.\n  - opencc-fmmseg pre-splits text on delimiters using a `DelimiterSet`\n    bitmask (u128 for ASCII, dense array for BMP).  Each segment is\n    converted independently, and delimiter runs are emitted verbatim.\n    This enables per-segment parallelism and eliminates cross-delimiter\n    false matches.\n  - For zhtw-mcp: pre-segmentation adds complexity (byte offset\n    tracking across segments, exclusion zone alignment) but enables\n    two wins:\n    1. Skip AC transitions on delimiter-only runs (~10-20% of typical\n       Markdown by character count).\n    2. Enable per-segment parallelism for large documents (>100KB)\n       via rayon, similar to opencc-fmmseg's `par_chunks()`.\n  - Risk: offset tracking bugs.  The scanner uses byte offsets\n    extensively (exclusion zones, line index, NFC normalization map).\n    Pre-segmentation must maintain a segment→original offset map.\n  - Profile first: if 30.7 profiling shows AC transition overhead on\n    delimiters is <5% of total scan time, close as won't-fix.  The\n    parallelism win is only relevant for documents >100KB, which are\n    rare in MCP usage (LLM context windows are ~32KB of text).\n  - Scope: `engine/scan/mod.rs` (pre-segmentation + offset map),\n    Cargo.toml (optional rayon dependency behind `parallel` feature).\n  - Gate: no regression on correctness (all existing tests pass).\n    Measurable speedup on 100KB+ input.  Parallelism gated behind\n    feature flag.",
